1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,056
Taxable is a crypto tax calculation and reporting project for Japan and the United States.

2
00:00:06,056 --> 00:00:11,304
This walkthrough covers the current specifications, screens, and user journey.

3
00:00:11,304 --> 00:00:13,794
Everything shown uses synthetic data.

4
00:00:13,794 --> 00:00:15,880
It is not ready for tax filing.

5
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:20,374
The web app and Expo mobile apps share an API client.

6
00:00:20,374 --> 00:00:26,836
The API stores imported transactions and append-only corrections, then runs the shared tax engine.

7
00:00:26,836 --> 00:00:30,066
Each personal session is limited to its own user.

8
00:00:30,066 --> 00:00:35,143
Rule packs are drafts, and exchange-specific importers still need validation.

9
00:00:36,143 --> 00:00:40,521
The actual web sign-in screen accepts a personal, expiring session.

10
00:00:40,521 --> 00:00:45,682
This is an invite-only development flow, not self-service account registration.

11
00:00:45,682 --> 00:00:47,904
The session is checked by the API.

12
00:00:47,904 --> 00:00:50,909
No session token is visible in this recording.

13
00:00:51,909 --> 00:00:58,383
After sign-in, the overview shows the selected country, year, calculation method, and draft totals.

14
00:00:58,383 --> 00:01:02,633
These synthetic records deliberately contain missing information.

15
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:09,826
Notice the unknown-impact badge: a zero uncertainty range does not mean those issues have no financial effect.

16
00:01:10,826 --> 00:01:15,320
The Import screen accepts a CSV and an exchange or wallet account name.

17
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:19,119
The format can be detected automatically or chosen manually.

18
00:01:19,119 --> 00:01:21,651
Uploads are limited to twenty megabytes.

19
00:01:21,651 --> 00:01:28,552
Here we use the documented canonical format, rather than claiming an unverified exchange export is validated.

20
00:01:29,552 --> 00:01:33,758
This is the result of a real upload to the disposable local API.

21
00:01:33,758 --> 00:01:38,292
The app reports imported rows, skipped rows, warnings, and conflicts.

22
00:01:38,292 --> 00:01:41,578
Review these results before relying on the ledger.

23
00:01:41,578 --> 00:01:45,653
No private financial information or external account was used.

24
00:01:46,653 --> 00:01:53,965
Transactions shows the normalized ledger: when each event occurred, which account it belongs to, and what was sent or received.

25
00:01:53,965 --> 00:01:57,766
Source identifiers connect the result back to the imported record.

26
00:01:57,766 --> 00:02:01,912
This screen helps check whether the data matches the original statement.

27
00:02:02,912 --> 00:02:05,671
The Fix queue surfaces unresolved items.

28
00:02:05,671 --> 00:02:14,572
In this synthetic example, an Ethereum deposit lacks cost basis, an airdrop lacks a price, and a withdrawal needs classification.

29
00:02:14,572 --> 00:02:19,953
Suggestions are aids for review, not verified facts about the user’s finances.

30
00:02:20,953 --> 00:02:26,813
Here the user enters a total cost basis of one hundred fifty thousand yen for the synthetic deposit.

31
00:02:26,813 --> 00:02:29,216
An acquisition date can also be supplied.

32
00:02:29,216 --> 00:02:35,662
The correction is recorded separately from the imported history, so the underlying source record is preserved.

33
00:02:36,662 --> 00:02:41,533
After saving, the missing-basis item is resolved and the report is recalculated.

34
00:02:41,533 --> 00:02:43,481
The other issues remain visible.

35
00:02:43,481 --> 00:02:51,580
The correction history records what changed, and the user can revoke a correction instead of silently deleting the original evidence.

36
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:58,266
The Report screen presents country-specific draft summaries, disposals, income, and holdings.

37
00:02:58,266 --> 00:03:02,240
These are engine results from the current ledger and corrections.

38
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:08,538
They are not an approved tax return and should not be treated as professionally reviewed filing advice.

39
00:03:09,538 --> 00:03:13,318
The report’s detail view makes calculations reviewable.

40
00:03:13,318 --> 00:03:19,502
It exposes proceeds, costs, cost basis, gain, and available provenance or rule references.

41
00:03:19,502 --> 00:03:27,955
These references explain the draft implementation; they do not substitute for professional validation of the tax treatment.

42
00:03:28,955 --> 00:03:35,791
Settings lets the user select jurisdiction, tax year, supported calculation method, and interface language.

43
00:03:35,791 --> 00:03:38,857
The web interface supports Japanese and English.

44
00:03:38,857 --> 00:03:48,121
Choosing a method changes the calculation configuration, but does not establish that the method is appropriate for a particular filing situation.

45
00:03:49,121 --> 00:03:52,575
Signing out revokes the current session through the API.

46
00:03:52,575 --> 00:03:54,425
The screen returns to sign-in.

47
00:03:54,425 --> 00:03:58,372
This action was exercised against the disposable local database.

48
00:03:58,372 --> 00:04:04,415
Other devices have their own sessions, and a full device-management interface remains future work.

49
00:04:05,415 --> 00:04:12,962
The following mobile layouts are illustrations based on the implemented Expo screens, not simulator or device captures.

50
00:04:12,962 --> 00:04:16,134
Mobile sign-in uses the same personal session API.

51
00:04:16,134 --> 00:04:18,670
Credentials are stored with SecureStore.

52
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:23,934
If logout is interrupted, the app persists the pending revocation and stays locked.

53
00:04:24,934 --> 00:04:30,902
The mobile app has four main areas: Report, Transactions, Import, and Corrections.

54
00:04:30,902 --> 00:04:37,597
Users can browse a paginated ledger, choose a CSV, inspect draft results, and review issues.

55
00:04:37,597 --> 00:04:45,893
Both iOS and Android JavaScript bundles build, but native binaries and device behavior have not yet been verified.

56
00:04:46,893 --> 00:04:55,196
Mobile also supports manual prices, total cost basis with acquisition date, and withdrawal classification or proceeds.

57
00:04:55,196 --> 00:04:58,644
The user reviews a correction before applying it.

58
00:04:58,644 --> 00:05:01,177
Source notes and undo are supported.

59
00:05:01,177 --> 00:05:08,284
Draft report details expose proceeds, basis, acquisition dates, source records, and rule identifiers.

60
00:05:09,284 --> 00:05:19,053
The preview journey is sign in, import, inspect the ledger, review issues, supply corrections, and inspect the recalculated report before signing out.

61
00:05:19,053 --> 00:05:26,478
Automated integration tests exercise these steps against the real API handlers and an in-memory Postgres database.

62
00:05:26,478 --> 00:05:29,409
Device testing is a separate remaining stage.

63
00:05:30,409 --> 00:05:35,547
This is a substantial development preview, not a release-ready filing product.

64
00:05:35,547 --> 00:05:47,602
Remaining work includes verified identity-provider login and recovery, native builds and device testing, security review, real exchange-export validation, and professional tax review.

65
00:05:47,602 --> 00:05:51,950
The video captures local uncommitted work, not a deployed release.
